{{also|Amplitude}}
==English==

{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|amplitude|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|amplitudo||lang=la}}, from {{term|amplus||large|lang=la}}; see [[ample]] and [[-itude]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈæmplɪtud/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-amplitude.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[measure]] of something's [[size]], especially in terms of [[width]] or [[breadth]]; [[largeness]], [[magnitude]].
#* Fuller
#*: The cathedral of Lincoln {{...}} is a magnificent structure, proportionable to the '''amplitude''' of the diocese.
#* Milton
#*: '''amplitude''' of mind
#* Macaulay
#*: '''amplitude''' of comprehension
# {{context|mathematics|lang=en}} The [[maximum]] [[absolute]] [[value]] of the [[vertical]] [[component]] of a [[curve]] or [[function]], especially one that is [[periodic]].
# {{context|physics|lang=en}} The maximum absolute value of some quantity that [[varies]].

